tdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkmKolina Koltaiƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;KGRWikm1X7glhT6x0tT-XLp59yn2ZOT0QUTQmBtqp2Aƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx#https://www.bellingcat.com/?p=53207ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;dP1flXndK-revIGrzeGsFa9HtOKTW5eApRWbGlES3poƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxThu, 16 Jul 2026 16:00:01 +0000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_knznWfta_uZ4VZbqEnGPlDUsHGpw9ldwI73CLKiZSwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkcrssƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Y5Dz-nKfL6CWwCUEK4drH7ZdA47kBQj-3ZS27hs174Eƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyƒhThis investigation is a collaboration between Bellingcat and New Zealand news site The Press. You can read The Pressâ€™ piece here .&nbsp; Content warning: This article discusses non-consensual sexually explicit content and child sexual abuse material. In 2024, a former track and field coach in Boston, Massachusetts, was sentenced to five years in jail for attempting to trick and extort more than 100 women,&nbsp;including student-athletes he coached, into sending him intimate photos.&nbsp; According to the 2021 criminal complaint , Steve Waithe stole photos from some of the student-athletesâ€™ phones under the pretence of â€œfilming their formâ€ at practices and meets. He also approached some victims via fake online accounts, telling them he had found their images on a forum site called â€œleakedbb.comâ€ (â€œLeakedBBâ€) and offering to help them remove these photos if they provided more images for â€œreferenceâ€.&nbsp; Authorities said Waithe also hired and paid another man &nbsp;in October 2020 to hack into the Snapchat accounts of women he coached or had other relationships with in an effort to steal and distribute nude images online.&nbsp; In one post, according to the US Attorneyâ€™s Office , Waithe wrote: â€œDoes anyone want to trade nudes? Iâ€™m talking girls you actually know. Could be exes or whatever. I have quite a few and [am] down to trade over snap[chat] or something.â€&nbsp; Legal documents do not name the sites on which Waithe distributed these images, but Bellingcat found a cached version of a November 2020 post with that exact wording on LeakedBB â€“ the same site he allegedly used to try to trick victims. Another cached LeakedBB thread posted a few months later shows the same user offering to trade nudes of athletes, including â€œa lot that I actually knowâ€. Screengrab from LeakedBB, showing a user asking to trade nudes of â€œgirls you actually knowâ€; redaction by Bellingcat Such posts were not unusual on the site: multiple archived pages show the forumâ€™s users either requesting Snapchat hacks or offering to help others hack Snapchat accounts, sometimes for a fee.&nbsp; In the criminal case against Waithe, the ownership of LeakedBB is never discussed, but a Bellingcat investigation can reveal that payment streams, company records and website domain information appear to lead back to one individual: Jitendra Maharaj, a Christchurch-based former pilot and co-founder of a cryptocurrency start-up, Pay It Now (PIN), which reportedly billed itself as the â€œStripe of crypto paymentsâ€.&nbsp; Support Bellingcat Your donations directly contribute to our ability to publish groundbreaking investigations and uncover wrongdoing around the world. Donate Our New Zealand publishing partner The Press sent an email to Maharaj on June 4 outlining our findings in detail and inviting him to respond. Maharaj did not reply to this.&nbsp; However, by June 6, LeakedBB was down . As of publication, the website remains inaccessible.&nbsp; The Press later received an email from a Christchurch-based lawyer representing Maharaj, who said their client was in Fiji for a family memberâ€™s funeral. The lawyer requested that we wait until his return on June 23.&nbsp; When The Press visited his residence â€“ a two-storey family home in Christchurchâ€™s affluent Aidanfield suburb â€“ on June 25, Maharaj said he did not know who was behind LeakedBB or operated it and that he was not sure if the website was down.&nbsp; He said he did not respond to the email queries and had not spoken to his lawyers about them because â€œall the evidence against me just sounded really weirdâ€ and it seemed like there was â€œsome kind of targeted attack out on meâ€ based on â€œmanufactured evidence or something thatâ€™s pointing me to this garbageâ€. However, he refused to comment on the record about most of the specific evidence linking him to the site and referred these questions to his lawyer.&nbsp; He also claimed that he had been contacted by people â€œtrying to harass me to get me to send them moneyâ€, but declined to provide details on the record. Jitendra Maharaj at the entrance of his residence on June 25, 2026. Source: Iain McGregor/The Press Despite a further extension of the deadline until July 6 â€“&nbsp;more than a month after we first reached out â€“ Maharaj and his lawyer had not provided any statement directly addressing the specific evidence linking him to the site as of publication.&nbsp; PIN, the company Maharaj co-founded, did not respond to The Press â€™ requests for comment. However, there is no suggestion that PIN has any knowledge of or involvement in LeakedBB. Profiting Off â€˜Leaksâ€™ LeakedBB was set up in 2019 and built a sizeable following over the next seven years, averaging an estimated two million visits a month from March to May this year. The board statistics shown on LeakedBBâ€™s homepage in May displayed 2.2 million registered users and more than 2.6 million posts. Screengrab of the boardâ€™s statistics as of May 26, 2026; personal information redacted by Bellingcat Googleâ€™s Transparency Report shows it received more than 95,000 individual requests for over 350,000 pages on LeakedBB to be delisted from search results. This resulted in Google de-listing more than 169,000 pages from its search results, according to the report.&nbsp; Shortly after the site went offline, a Reddit post noting the outage and asking for alternatives trended in the â€œhotâ€ section of a piracy subreddit, accumulating almost 700 votes in a week. In response to a question by one commenter asking what the site was, another person replied: â€œNot only did it have â€˜onlyfansâ€™ content, also amateur, asian, arabic, celebrity and other hacked phone/icloud content from other sites.â€ This comment accurately summarised some of the content on the site. In LeakedBBâ€™s early days , it had sections for other types of â€œleakedâ€ content such as computer programmes and eBooks. But within months, the forumâ€™s discussions were almost exclusively about pornographic images and videos that members claimed had been leaked â€“&nbsp;implying that it was non-consensual, hacked or stolen content. The most popular section on the forum contained content that claimed to be from sites such as OnlyFans and Fansly, which, if shared without the original creatorsâ€™ consent, would be a violation of their intellectual property .&nbsp; Reba Rocket, co-owner and chief operating officer of Takedown Piracy, a company that helps both adult performers and private individuals remove non-consensually shared explicit media, said sites like LeakedBB cause financial harm to legitimate content creators. â€œPeople would not shove a DVD into their coat pocket and walk out of the store â€“&nbsp;thatâ€™s something tangible that they know theyâ€™re doing something wrong, whereas watching something on the internet for free doesnâ€™t have that same connected moral,â€ she said.&nbsp; Other sections on LeakedBB featured threads requesting or promoting content that often appeared to show women who did not have anything to do with the adult industry, which the posts claimed were leaked, hacked or even obtained through blackmail.&nbsp; One post advertised images of girls from 29 US states: â€œThereâ€™s names and Facebook information if you want that,â€ the member, â€œMaster Leakerâ€, posted. â€œThereâ€™s also two girls that got blackmailed into sending more nudes as well!â€ LeakedBB user advertising a large file of â€œgirls from 29 different statesâ€; personal information redacted by Bellingcat In the â€œRequestsâ€ section, users shared clothed images of women or social media handles of potential victims, and asked if others had leaked content of them. In one recent post looking for a â€œFlorida Milfâ€, a user wrote: â€œShe may go by the name [redacted]. Looks like the daughter graduated from [redacted]. Anyone have content of her? Sex tapes?â€&nbsp; Some users also posted nude or intimate images of women they had found elsewhere, asking for help finding out their real identities. â€œWho is she?â€ or â€œCan anyone ID?â€ were some common questions in the posts.&nbsp; Non-consensual intimate image sharing (NCII), colloquially referred to as â€œ revenge porn â€, is far from new. It is a known problem on Reddit, where, in 2022, a BBC investigation found â€œthousandsâ€ of such images being shared despite the platformâ€™s attempts to crack down on the issue.&nbsp; LeakedBB, however, seemed to take the opposite approach: instead of trying to moderate or prevent users from posting what appeared to be NCII, it sought to profit from and reward it.&nbsp; Except for preview images, most of the content users shared in the â€œleaksâ€ section was behind a paywall and could only be accessed with memberships costing up to US$99.99 or by redeeming credits.&nbsp; The site rewarded members with credits for posting â€œleakedâ€ content, as well as when other members spent credits to â€œunlockâ€ their content. These credits could be used to access links that users could otherwise only view with a paid upgrade, or redeemed for cryptocurrency at varying rates (the most frequent contributors had the option to cash out the equivalent of up to $0.15 for each thread they posted).&nbsp; There was also an annual Christmas contest, with last yearâ€™s total prizes worth over $4,000 in cryptocurrency for users who posted or liked the most threads. Screengrab of a forum announcement on LeakedBB posted on Dec. 7, 2025. Rocket said LeakedBB had â€œdamaged many peopleâ€, including clients of her company. â€œThose specific clients are not in the adult industry,â€ she said, â€œbut LeakedBB seemed more than happy to share their non-consensual contentâ€.&nbsp; The â€œleaksâ€ were often posted with womenâ€™s purported real names, locations and social media accounts, as well as preview images showing their uncovered faces. One poster said sharing a womanâ€™s social media details â€œadds to the experienceâ€.&nbsp; â€œFor me, it makes my jerk-off sesh feel more personal, as if sheâ€™s an actual person I know rather than a moviestar/pornstar,â€ the post said. Screengrab of a post where a LeakedBB user shared content of a woman, including her socials; personal information redacted by Bellingcat There were more than 80 responses to this thread, mostly thanking the original poster for sharing the content. One of them, however, claimed to be the woman shown in the images: â€œPlease remove this link. These photos were illegally stolen from me. This constitutes revenge porn and violates US law. Police are already involved. Not only is it illegal but just gross.â€ Allison Mahoney, the founder and managing attorney at ALM Law in New York and Colorado, told Bellingcat she received calls about cases involving NCII â€œall the timeâ€.&nbsp; â€œIt kind of amazes me, given the amount of media attention this has gotten over the years, that people are still engaging in this type of abuse so cavalierly,â€ said Mahoney, whose firm specialises in providing legal services for abuse survivors and children harmed in welfare systems. Mahoney and Rocket agreed that sites sharing NCII often had real-world implications for victims, especially when images were posted alongside personal information, including names, contact information and professions.&nbsp; â€œWe have clients who â€¦ their children were kicked out of Catholic school, or they lost their mainstream job, or relationships ended, or families cut them off simply because content was posted online without their consent and viewed by others,â€ Rocket said.&nbsp; Mahoney said online abuse can turn into offline abuse when victims have their personal information, like their name, profession and contact information, posted with their images.&nbsp; She has seen clients who had strangers show up at their homes or places of work, threatening their physical safety â€“ a situation she said was â€œreally terrifyingâ€.&nbsp; In July last year, LeakedBB closed a marketplace it had hosted for more than five years, which allowed users to sell leaks and services to each other. Lucifer NightStar, the administrator account on the site, said there were allegations of people selling â€œUA [underage] materialâ€, which was â€œnot something we want on [LeakedBB]â€. Screengrab of a post where Lucifer NightStar explained why the marketplace section had been shut down. On one section of the forum, which was specifically for sharing content from other sites that hosted leaked pornographic content, LeakedBB had a disclaimer: â€œPlease note that posting any content on any one below the legal age of 18 is against the law. We have a zero tolerance policy on such things and your account will immediately be banned / reported.â€ But this warning did not appear on other sections of the forum, including those featuring threads of â€œamateur nudesâ€ described as having been leaked. Some threads on the forum, which remained accessible shortly before the entire site was taken down, also described images of â€œyoung teensâ€.&nbsp; While it is not known if those descriptions are accurate, in a recent post on Reddit a person asked for help taking down non-consensual photos they said were taken when they were a minor, hacked from Snapchat, and posted on LeakedBB, among other sites.&nbsp; â€œI am in school to become a teacher and searched my name on google. If you go down a bit these websites come up,â€ they wrote. â€œI am so devastated and canâ€™t believe this has happened to me.â€&nbsp; While speaking to The Press outside his residence on June 25, Maharaj said that when it came to publishing non-consensual pornography and child sexual abuse imagery, â€œIt should be obvious anyoneâ€™s against that.â€ Who Is Lucifer NightStar? Lucifer NightStar was the username for the only account with the title of â€œAdministratorâ€ on the LeakedBB forum. This user posted FAQs for the site and almost every forum announcement throughout its history.&nbsp; The URL of this accountâ€™s profile page shows the user ID (UID) of â€œ1â€. According to documentation for MyBB , a free and open source forum software that LeakedBB has credited for powering the site, the first user of the forum is assigned the UID â€œ1â€ and has super administrator privileges â€“&nbsp;meaning their account cannot be deleted, banned or otherwise altered by regular administrators. While the profile did not state the userâ€™s location, it did show a local timestamp based on the userâ€™s timezone settings, which matched GMT+12 â€“&nbsp;a timezone used in several countries in Oceania, including New Zealand and Fiji.&nbsp; Lucifer NightStarâ€™s recent posts generally avoid mentioning non-consensual intimate imagery, focusing on administrative updates and issues, troubleshooting and the annual Christmas contest. However, in the first few months of the forumâ€™s existence, the user posted a thread with a â€œLeakedBB Exclusiveâ€ of â€œleaked Kiwi girlsâ€. One of Lucifer NightStarâ€™s first posts on LeakedBB, sharing content described as â€œleaked Kiwi girlsâ€. In another discussion thread from 2020, Lucifer NightStar vouched for a userâ€™s ability to â€œinfluenceâ€ another memberâ€™s ex-girlfriend to share nudes. (Top) LeakedBB user offering their services to obtain nudes from another userâ€™s ex-partner; (Below) A response from Lucifer NightStar vouching for this user being a â€œpremium collectorâ€. Personal information redacted by Bellingcat Maharaj did not respond to The Press and Bellingcatâ€™s question about whether he was Lucifer NightStar. However, one of the administratorâ€™s posts led us to a clue pointing to Maharajâ€™s possible connection with LeakedBB.&nbsp; Logica Ltd and MyBBplugins In one post in May 2021, responding to a user reporting problems paying with Apple Pay, Lucifer NightStar shared a screenshot of what the payment screen should look like. A company name was visible in this image: â€œLogica LTDâ€. Screenshot of a forum post by Lucifer NightStar on how to pay for a premium upgrade for LeakedBB using Apple Pay, showing the company name â€œLogica LTDâ€. New Zealand company records show that Logica Limited was registered by Maharaj in February 2021, just months before this post. The company address is also in Christchurch, where Maharaj lives. (Note: This is a different company from Logica Partners Limited, based in Auckland, which has no apparent connection with Maharaj or LeakedBB and is unrelated to this investigation.)&nbsp; The records from the New Zealand Companies Office show that Maharaj has been the sole director of Logica Limited since its incorporation. He stated on his LinkedIn profile that he was self-employed as the CEO of Logica NZ from May 2020 to August 2021.&nbsp; (Maharajâ€™s LinkedIn profile appears to have been deleted between June 13 and June 15, after The Press and Bellingcatâ€™s initial enquiries and during the period his lawyer said he was in Fiji attending a family memberâ€™s funeral.) Left: Screengrab of Jitendraâ€™s work history from LinkedIn; right: Company registration information for Logica Limited (redaction by Bellingcat). Sources: LinkedIn, New Zealand Companies Office But that was not the only connection to Logica Limited. On May 4, 2022, a YouTube user with the display name â€œLeakedBBâ€ uploaded a video on how to pay for memberships on the site. This video was also embedded on LeakedBBâ€™s homepage.&nbsp; The video showed how users could pay by credit card. When they clicked to purchase a membership, LeakedBB would redirect them to another website to buy a digital avatar pack with a price corresponding to their selected membership tier.&nbsp; After purchasing this â€œreferral productâ€, users were encouraged to leave a comment and a positive rating to receive an â€œextra bonus monthâ€. Archived versions of the website show view counts in the tens of thousands for some of these avatar packs.&nbsp; The thumbnails of the â€œdigital avatarsâ€ as well as their price and description, as shown in the video, were identical to those shown on the archived version of a site , logica.nz, which is recorded as Logica Limitedâ€™s website on OpenCorporates. This site also lists â€œLogica LTDâ€ in its copyright information at the bottom of its landing page .&nbsp; (Bellingcat last accessed a live version of the video on June 15. By July 1, we noticed that the video had been removed by the uploader.) Left: YouTube video on how to purchase upgrades on LeakedBB. Right: Archived purchase screen of the same avatar pack on Logica.nz In a forum thread on LeakedBB dedicated to explaining alternative ways to pay for membership, hundreds of users posted that they had just purchased the â€œMystic Avatar Packâ€ or the â€œPixel Avatar Packâ€ to gain access to the site. One user included screenshots of their purchase, showing the site URL to be â€œlogica.nzâ€. Other users also stated that they had made the purchase on this website and were waiting to receive their upgrades. Shared screenshot from a LeakedBB user who purchased a â€œPixel Avatarâ€ pack in exchange for membership, showing that they left a comment, like other users, on the purchase page on logica.nz . Personal information redacted by Bellingcat This websiteâ€™s landing page now displays only a note stating that it is under maintenance. However, according to archives captured by the Internet Archive, it was still selling â€œdigital avatar packsâ€ in March 2025. This type of payment structure not only conceals the nature of the transaction from the payment processor (as non-consensual content violates most platformsâ€™ terms of service), but it also hides the transactions for the user, as payments are not described as being made to â€œLeakedBBâ€ on bank statements. When asked about the links between LeakedBB and Logica Limited, Maharaj only told The Press at the doorstep interview on June 25 that â€œLogica was my company. I cannot say what happened there right nowâ€. According to the New Zealand Companies Register, Logica Limited is in good standing, with its most recent annual filing submitted by Maharaj in March 2026.&nbsp; The Domain Name System (DNS) records of LeakedBB revealed another connection that seems to point back to Maharaj. Using online investigations tool DNSlytics , we viewed DNS records for the website and found that in 2020, the MX (mail exchange) record for LeakedBB.com was set to LeakedBB.net. An MX record is the mail server set up to accept emails for that domain. For LeakedBB.com, this was later changed to ProtonMail.&nbsp; While the WHOIS ownership of LeakedBB.net is obscured, we found it on a list of sites that had DNS certificates issued by another site, mybbplugins.com. A DNS certificate is used to prove ownership of a domain and requires an administrator to validate that certificate.&nbsp; According to WHOIS records from cyberthreat intelligence platform DomainTools , mybbplugins.com was publicly registered to Maharaj from December 2011 to February 2019, after which the registrant information was redacted. The same site also issued a DNS certificate for a domain bearing Maharajâ€™s name (jitendramaharaj.com) as well as two domains that include part of his first name, jit-pay.cc and thejitshow.com. DNS certificates for these domains were issued between 2016 and 2021, according to free Certificate Transparency monitoring site crt.sh . Both â€œleakedbbâ€ and the domain names linked to Maharajâ€™s name (i.e. â€œjitendramaharajâ€, â€œjit-payâ€ and â€œthejitshowâ€) were also used as subdomains for mybbplugins.com, records from DomainTools show.&nbsp; Another link appeared when we inspected the code of the oldest saved archive of the payment screen on LeakedBB, from November 2019, which showed a ProtonMail address associated with the PayPal form at the time with a string of seven digits as the username.&nbsp; This string of seven digits is an exact match for what appears to be part of a Fiji-based phone number listed on WHOIS records for websites registered to Maharajâ€™s name including mybbplugins.com , from 2008 to 2011. It is unclear whether Maharaj was using this phone number in 2019, by the time LeakedBB was set up, and a different Fiji-based phone number was used with his name when the registration for mybbplugins.com was renewed in 2016. Top: The archived HTML code for LeakedBBâ€™s payment page, with a ProtonMail email linked to its PayPal account. Bottom: The WHOIS domain registry for mybbplugins.com, registered to Maharaj in 2011, with a phone number matching the digits to the ProtonMail email. Graphic: Galen Reich Explore some of the links between Maharaj and LeakedBB: Graphic: Galen Reich From MyBB to LeakedBB Bellingcat also found several other apparent connections between Maharaj and other applications hosting adult content.&nbsp; An account with the username â€œJitendra M.â€ has been posting on the MyBB community forum since 2008, with the account ID originally using the username â€œDarkmewâ€. An archived capture of this accountâ€™s profile information showed a date of birth and a location in Fiji.&nbsp; This date of birth matches the one listed on a Facebook profile Bellingcat found under Maharajâ€™s name. His LinkedIn profile also shows that prior to moving to Christchurch, he worked in Nadi, Fiji, and he has listed addresses in the city for some of the domains registered to his name, as well as an email with a Fijian domain. This user also mentions that they are a pilot .&nbsp; Jitendra M.â€™s profile bears a â€œformer staffâ€ label, indicating that he used to work for MyBB. A previous commit (save) of a file containing details of MyBB team members shows that the full name associated with this accountâ€™s user ID and username was â€œJitendra Maharajâ€, and his website was listed as jitendra-maharaj.com.&nbsp; Archived versions of this site show photos and details that match those from Maharajâ€™s public social media profiles and interviews. For example, a 2011 capture shows that he mentioned being a pilot at a company called Pacific Sun. Pacific Sun was later rebranded as Fiji Link , and Maharajâ€™s LinkedIn profile, before it was deleted, stated that he worked for Fiji Link from 2009 to 2015. A blog post on the site also refers to mybbplugins.com as the authorâ€™s â€œnewest endeavourâ€. Left: Archived profile of â€œDarkmewâ€â€™s profile on MyBB; Right: Screengrab of information from a Facebook profile under Maharajâ€™s name, which has either been made private or deleted as of publication. Very shortly after joining the MyBB community forum in Feb 2008, Jitendra M. asked about using MyBB for â€œwarezâ€ (an internet slang term for pirated digital content) and/or adult content. He stated that he was â€œinterested in using it for a [sic] adult forumâ€.&nbsp; During this time, he also posted asking about streaming videos from a server and how to use a PayPal account without a credit card for â€œpeople putting money into my account for services I provideâ€. In late 2008, Jitendra M. purchased a web domain, reaperscrypt.info, which Wayback Machine archives show hosted pornographic content while it was online in 2009. This domain was publicly registered to Maharaj from November 2008 to January 2010.&nbsp; In 2013, he posted about selling the mybbplugins.com domain. However, as previously mentioned, Maharajâ€™s name was still publicly registered as the owner of the domain until February 2019, when registration data was redacted. Top: Post by Jitendra M. about using MyBB for warez and adult sites using MyBB; Bottom: Post about selling mybbplugins.com Bellingcat was able to view Facebook and Instagram accounts under Maharajâ€™s name and showing his profile picture in early May. These accounts painted a picture of a family man, with his public photos mainly showing his wife and children. His Facebook account had been either deleted or made private by May, and his Instagram account, while still active, has not been updated since 2013. Archives of an X account using the same username as Maharajâ€™s Facebook and Instagram accounts also show several posts from November 2019 promoting LeakedBB.&nbsp; Archived tweets from an account, using the same username as what appeared to be Maharajâ€™s former Facebook and Instagram accounts, which posted links to LeakedBB in November 2019. Personal information redacted by Bellingcat The â€œDarkmewâ€ username that Jitendra M. originally used was also used for a GitHub account which hosts a repository described as the â€œofficial repository for Pay it Now â€“ PIN Tokenâ€. This account, which now redirects to an account with the username â€œJitMaharajâ€, has also forked (or copied) two apps created by other people: one to create a subscription platform â€œlike onlyfans.comâ€ that uses cryptocurrency for payments; the other designed to scrape and report illicit content from LeakedBB. Screengrabs from the â€œJitMaharajâ€ GitHub account, which forked repositories for an application designed to create a platform â€œlike â€˜onlyfans.comâ€™, and another to report illicit content from LeakedBB. These forked repositories were among 38 visible on JitMaharajâ€™s account on June 17, but by July 1 â€“&nbsp;after a June 22 query from The Press asking Maharaj whether he owned this account â€“ there were only 25 repositories listed on this account. The two repositories mentioned above were among those removed. Maharaj did not respond to questions about whether he owned any of the accounts or domains mentioned in this section. â€˜Hiding Behind Screensâ€™ Mahoney said that successfully removing clientsâ€™ images from platforms like LeakedBB was a time-consuming task. â€œSome sites, usually the sites hosted overseas, will just ignore the request and wonâ€™t take them down,â€ she said. In the US, which accounted for almost half ( 40 percent ) of LeakedBBâ€™s web traffic in May, the Take it Down Act recently came into effect. The new federal law requires platforms to quickly remove non-consensually shared intimate imagery when it is reported. However, there has been little discussion of the law on LeakedBB. One user asked in the â€œHelpâ€ forum how this act would affect the site and its members back in October 2025, but Lucifer NightStar never responded to this post. LeakedBB user asking about the Take It Down Act Rocket said having content removed for her US-based clients could be difficult when the platforms were based overseas: â€œA lot of it depends on where the platform is hosted, who runs their ad network and who is monetising â€“ who their payment processors are,â€ she said.&nbsp; LeakedBB accepted cryptocurrency payments through NOWPayments, a cryptocurrency payments gateway based in the Netherlands and Estonia. The purchase page for its subscription plans, which allowed users to gain unrestricted access to the site, redirected to a NOWPayments purchase screen to transfer cryptocurrency to LeakedBB.&nbsp; In response to questions from Bellingcat, NOWPayments confirmed that LeakedBBâ€™s activities violated its terms of service . The payments provider said it had deactivated LeakedBBâ€™s account and blacklisted the platform immediately, as of June 4.&nbsp; LeakedBB did have a form for people to request that their content be taken down under the Digital Millennium Copyright Act (DMCA), a US copyright law. However, this required victims to submit personal information such as a physical address and a business email address, and stated that it would reject requests that used email addresses from free services like Google and ProtonMail. Such details appear to go beyond those required for DMCA takedown requests on other sites: for example, Google only requires a first and last name, and an email address from any domain. In one Reddit thread discussing the difficulty of removing content from LeakedBB under the DMCA, someone commented: â€œSome of this seems fairly standard, some of it seems like itâ€™s designed to make people not request a takedown for fear of doxing [sic] themselves.â€ On the page to submit DMCA takedown requests, LeakedBB also stated that successful requests would lead to them removing content hosted on their servers, but not links to third-party hosting providers â€“ which is how a large portion of the content was made available to the websiteâ€™s users.&nbsp; In New Zealand, where Maharaj is based, posting intimate imagery without consent is illegal under the Harmful Digital Communications Act . People face up to two years imprisonment or a fine up to NZ$50,000 (US$29,200), while for a company, the fine can be as high as NZ$200,000.&nbsp; Netsafe is the only approved body in the country that handles complaints under this act. The agencyâ€™s chief online safety officer Sean Lyons told The Press that the law was quite novel and other jurisdictions were â€œenviousâ€ when it was enacted â€“ it was able to respond to generative AI technology that didnâ€™t exist when it was written, and gave New Zealand courts powers to issue takedown orders, even in other countries. Related articles by Bellingcat Europe Profiting From Exploitation: How We Found the Man Behind Two Deepfake Porn Sites Still, Lyons said the law had its limitations: it was mostly intended for use where one individual was harming another, and if the responsible party was overseas, the lawâ€™s efficacy largely relied on responsible platforms doing the right thing.&nbsp; â€œThere are times when within our process, we will have contacted platforms or hosts and they will have said, â€˜Who the heck are you?â€™â€¦[Or] â€˜We know what weâ€™re doing, we are quite comfortable with what we are doing, and we donâ€™t give a stuff about what it is that you are telling us, or about New Zealand law, or about the harm.â€™â€ Mahoney and Rocket agreed that current laws were limited in their effectiveness against sites like LeakedBB.&nbsp; â€œThe fact of the matter is there are places where â€¦ until there is an enforceable international law, that content is going to be available forever, which means there is a risk of it being shared forever,â€ Rocket said.&nbsp; Mahoney said image-based abusers have also become more sophisticated over time: â€œTechnology is advancing, and the law is always playing catch-up,â€ she said.&nbsp; But she suggested that identifying those responsible for the abuse could have a deterrent effect: â€œThe anonymity that people have hiding behind screens really contributes to this and emboldens people to act in ways that are very abusive to people. â€œIf people understand that thereâ€™s a risk that their identity and their bad behaviour will be revealed, the hope is that it will curtail some of this and dissuade people from engaging in this type of conduct, which is so, so harmful to the victims.â€&nbsp; If you are a victim or know anyone who is affected by image-based abuse, resources and support are available through StopNCII.org . Galen Reich and Melissa Zhu from Bellingcat and Michael Wright from The Press contributed to this article.&nbsp; Bellingcat is a non-profit and the ability to carry out our work is dependent on the kind support of individual donors. If you would like to support our work, you can do so&nbsp; here . You can also subscribe to our Patreon channel&nbsp; here . Subscribe to our&nbsp; Newsletter &nbsp;and follow us on Bluesky&nbsp; here , Instagram&nbsp; here , Reddit&nbsp; here&nbsp; and YouTube&nbsp; here . The post Identifying the Crypto Entrepreneur Linked to Popular Forum Trading in â€˜Leakedâ€™ Nudes of Women appeared first on bellingcat .ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;GVqKR_Of3yjOcc_N5vrePY3wYGfjWcbY5_o5X4e_QTMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxbIdentifying the Crypto Entrepreneur Linked to Popular Forum Trading in â€˜Leakedâ€™ Nudes of Womenƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;xaMjXomcFN0FSOJKsZM8ZIK0HPEGrLe0U5RnMyrqapwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;s4fRZ7zQTPAeWOAn4oVz6j3y6SrZ-AqrCTu1EY4KiA4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxHhttps://www.bellingcat.com/news/2026/07/16/leakedbb-crypto-entrepreneur/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;lqMatI-oqBQbileH4X_cCEbqK0La3RJrUz8ELi_xXmgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx\identifying-the-crypto-entrepreneur-linked-to-popular-forum-trading-in-leaked-nudes-of-womenƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zR3xdPnKueHtgmdWrePetUqHOuHtqzJriWMHc25Oeloƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkwSyndicationSourceBundleƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;538OtLgGbLGaB_KZARLgRvy5uNDiRZAtMjtvoq4rBP8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒeright